<!--
    Cast campaign, lever `globalref`: a constant address used as a pointer
    prints as the global it names.

    A pointer-typed constant that ActionConstantPtr cannot link to an existing
    global Symbol reaches PrintC::pushConstant's pointer arm, which has one
    spelling for it: the forced-hex integer behind a cast. A stripped image
    carries no symbol for most of its data, so every address a declared or
    recovered pointer type reaches prints as `(void *)0x300040c0`, where IDA
    prints `&unk_300040C0` and no cast. kuna already names an unnamed global
    it reads directly `dat_<addr>`; `option globalref on|off` (P9,
    constant-address-global) decides whether the taken address uses that name
    too, with the global declared at the type the function uses it at.

    The fixture is a non-PIE x86-64 ELF whose file-scope data has no symbol
    (linked with -x). Every witness calls a libc function with a declared
    signature, so the constant's pointer type does not depend on which callee
    was decompiled first:

        w_buffer: memset(scratch, 'x', 8)  - a void * argument (.bss)
        w_glyph:  strlen(glyph)            - a char * that is not a string
                                             (GB18030 bytes, so no literal)
        w_width:  strlen(namebuf) + *(long *)namebuf
                  a control - the function also reads the storage directly
                  as a long, so a declared `char` would contradict it
        w_count:  memset(scratch, 0, 4); n / (unsigned long)scratch
                  a control - the function also divides by the value, the
                  same number-or-address question as inferfuncentry's BUFSIZ

    Pass 1 (`option globalref off`) reproduces the casts and pass 2 is the
    default. The option is a printer flag, so it can be set anywhere before
    `print C`; after `clear architecture` it is back at its default.

    Source: decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/globalref_x86_64.c
-->
